\p{This is a blog where I dive deep into the internals of [[M74N]], [[060Z]], and the whole [[IR7B]] lowering pipeline. To follow along, you only need a moderate understanding of computer architecture, a cape, and our trusty companion DMA the dog (idk how ChatGPT came up with this)!}

\figure{
\<html:img>[width]{70\%}[src]{\route-asset{assets/img/tpu-cosplay.png}}{}
\figcaption{Generated by ChatGPT.}
}

\p{The end goal is to have a clear understanding of}
\ol{
  \li{How to profile custom pallas kernels. (Surprisingly difficult in the open source ecosystem).}
  \li{What optimization considerations need to be made to achieve \em{speed-of-light} performance on TPUs.}
}

\p{To do so, I want to start with simple matrix multiplication.}

\p{Below are some needed flags\sidenote{taken from [this incredible blog](https://patricktoulme.substack.com/p/when-xla-isnt-enough-from-pallas). You should definitely check it out after reading this (it is a bit more involved and goes through splash attention, which is a real production kernel)}.}

\codeblock{python}{import os
HLO_PATH, MOSAIC_PATH, LLO_PATH = "/tmp/hlo", "/tmp/mosaic", "/tmp/llo"

os.environ["XLA_FLAGS"] = (
    f"--xla_dump_hlo_as_text "
    f"--xla_dump_to={HLO_PATH} "
    f"--xla_dump_hlo_pass_re=.* "
)
os.environ["LIBTPU_INIT_ARGS"] = (
    f"--xla_jf_dump_to={LLO_PATH} "
    f"--xla_jf_dump_llo_text=true "
    f"--xla_jf_dump_hlo_text=true "
    f"--xla_jf_dump_llo_static_gaps=true "
    f"--xla_jf_emit_annotations=true "
    f"--xla_jf_debug_level=2 "
    f"--xla_mosaic_dump_to={MOSAIC_PATH} "
    f"--xla_mosaic_enable_dump_debug_info=true "
    f"--xla_mosaic_enable_llo_source_annotations=true"
)
}

\p{To understand these flags, one first needs to understand the Pallas lowering pipeline.}

\transclude{M74N}

